WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:01.740
[Music]

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:06.600
[The Federal Security Agency. Paul V. McNutt.
Administrator]

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:11.100
[The United States Public Health Service.
Thomas Parran. Surgeon General presents]

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.740
[Save a Day!]

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.020
[Narrator:] America's rear guard army!

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:24.200
They wear no uniforms and their ranks are
machinists, welder, riveter, instead of sergent,

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.420
lieutenant, and captain.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:32.930
But, they are an army. An army of 50 million
Americans doing the jobs that must be done

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:35.870
to make America strong for victory.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:41.720
Their health and strength, their ability to
produce, is the nation's most vital resource.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.720
These people are America's industrial army.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:51.200
Our resources must be protected, and here
protection starts, when the worker's day starts.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:57.320
It starts with the milk we drink. Most of
our milk is pasteurized and then bottled under

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:00.610
conditions that protect our health.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:06.540
The water that flows into most of our homes
comes from carefully guarded sources to avoid

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:12.000
pollution and is kept at high standards of
purity.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.580
The food we eat is inspected to protect our
health.

00:01:24.649 --> 00:01:30.619
Sewage disposal in most of our cities is scientific
and thorough to protect us against diseases

00:01:30.619 --> 00:01:35.340
that are a threat [sound skip] of unsanitary
conditions.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:41.140
And in hundreds of towns and cities, new homes
have been built under government sponsorship.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:48.140
Homes that let in air and sunlight. Homes
that help protect our health.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.340
These and many other precautions are taken,

00:01:50.349 --> 00:01:56.670
yet when the working army of America goes
to the factories and shipyards and mines,

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:01.130
a million men and women are missing.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:04.880
Every day, a million workers are off the job
because of sickness.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.149
It means that coal, which is needed, remains
in the earth.

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:15.149
Assembly lines are slowed down. Machines untended.
It means four hundred million days a year

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:18.010
junked.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:23.090
In these millions of days lost, 164,000 tanks
could be built.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:28.739
A line of tanks that would present a solid
front over 200 miles long.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.120
The reasons for some of this loss can be found
in industry itself.

00:02:34.129 --> 00:02:39.540
There are the old reasons. Some jobs are dangerous.
Some men are careless.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:46.239
There are many old industrial health problems,
not yet solved, and now there are new problems.

00:02:46.239 --> 00:02:51.709
New developments in industry, new machines
and materials are creating hazards that further

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:54.739
challenge safety control.

00:02:54.739 --> 00:03:00.170
And war production has brought the speed and
strain that spell: "danger."

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:05.140
These are conditions that keep tens of thousands
of men off the job every year.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:10.790
But, the hazards must be controlled. The men
must be kept on the job.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:15.069
So the question was asked, "How can the wasted
days be saved?"

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:17.999
This question was asked by some of the men
at the machines.

00:03:17.999 --> 00:03:23.019
It was asked by industry. And it was asked
by the United States Public Health Service.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.880
[Sign reads: Federal Security Agency. U.S. Public Health Service. National Institute of Health. National Cancer Institute.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:31.680
[Sign reads: Industrial Hygiene]

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:40.180
In the research section of the Public Health
Service, scientists are searching for the answer.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:46.859
They are searching for it in their laboratories with experiment and study. With equipment

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:50.129
and men geared to do the job.

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:53.870
They search for the understanding of metal
poisoning.

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:57.930
For ways to fight dust hazards and fatigue.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:01.550
They study the science of ventilation and
lighting.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.400
Every problem of worker's health is part of
their work.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:11.819
And the men who work here go to other government
agencies and to industry and help them

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:13.679
meet their industrial health problems.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.400
Here is the Division of Industrial Hygiene
at work on a research project,

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:25.710
a study of the hazards of changing pressures,
which affect tunnel workers and more important

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:29.330
to defense, aviators.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:36.320
In this pressure chamber, the temperature
and pressure of different altitudes

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:40.660
can be reproduced.

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:49.810
On solid ground, the effects of flight on
the flier's body can be observed and studied.

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:55.930
There is danger to the aviator in the swift
and sudden high climb of the plane and danger,

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:58.900
too, in the hard-driving power dive.

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:02.740
Out of this laboratory study, the division
hopes to take a weapon with which to fight

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:06.200
these dangers.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:14.340
The job of saving a day starts in the laboratory.
But it has to be finished in the plant.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:19.479
With the advice and assistance of the Public
Health Service and state health department,

00:05:19.479 --> 00:05:25.060
many industries are doing their part of the
job with programs of their own.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:31.440
They are introducing free employment examinations,
which tell them if a man is physically fit

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.140
for the job.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:43.560
And with a regular check-up, they make sure
that the men who are healthy, stay healthy.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.200
And those who need treatment, get it.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:53.580
Every one of these precautions help to save
a day for the worker and his family.

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:59.410
For industry. For the nation.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.960
And the well-run, well-equipped dispensaries
of industry save many days, too.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:14.379
They keep a cut from becoming a dangerous
infection, and a minor eye injury 

00:06:14.379 --> 00:06:15.680
from becoming blindness.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:21.250
And equipment, like the x-ray seen here,
helps diagnose the broken bones, the lung

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:26.150
infection, and points the way to treatment.

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:31.100
In studying the health needs of our industrial
army, the Public Health Service works with

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:32.699
other government agencies.

00:06:32.699 --> 00:06:36.819
For example, a call from the War Department
comes through to Dr. Townshend,

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:39.800
Chief of the Division of Industrial Hygiene,

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:44.289
asking that an investigation be made of government-built and government-operated arsenals.

00:06:44.289 --> 00:06:50.470
Dr. Townshend and Sanitary Engineer Bloomfield
discuss the problem.

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:53.330
They decide to put a field unit on the job.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:58.600
The decision made, testing apparatus is carefully
checked and then carefully loaded.

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:01.870
They take instruments for measuring ventilation,

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:07.639
instruments for determining dust concentration and for analyzing gases and vapor.

00:07:07.639 --> 00:07:13.949
It is not long from the time the call comes
to Dr. Townshend, to the time the doctor and

00:07:13.949 --> 00:07:17.860
engineer, the field unit crew, leave for the
arsenal.

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:23.669
Ready to start their work. Ready to track
down the cause of a high accident rate,

00:07:23.669 --> 00:07:25.970
or a strange skin rash,

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:31.169
or the feelings of dizziness that have suddenly
attacked a group of workers.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:37.180
The health forces of the nation unite with
the production forces to protect the health

00:07:37.189 --> 00:07:38.530
of the workers.

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:43.009
To save a day, wherever possible.

00:07:43.009 --> 00:07:47.889
And there are industrial hygiene units which
render similar services to industries

00:07:47.889 --> 00:07:48.879
throughout the nation.

00:07:48.879 --> 00:07:53.619
Units which are maintained by the state health
department in cooperation with the

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:56.159
Public Health Service.

00:07:56.159 --> 00:08:03.159
The men of a unit search out hazards and find
a way of removing them.

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:12.669
Dry drilling -- there is danger here.

00:08:12.669 --> 00:08:17.770
The engineer takes a sample of the dust-laden
atmosphere to find out exactly

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:19.889
how great the danger is.

00:08:19.889 --> 00:08:24.000
This is a type of dust that leaves scars on
men's lungs,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.319
that may bring on tuberculosis, that sometimes takes men's lives.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.940
Then, back in a hotel room only a few miles
from the mine, the engineers set up their

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:38.240
equipment and make their tests.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:44.370
These tests will determine the number of dust
particles, their size, and their nature.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:49.390
On the basis of this information, the extent
of the hazard is measured and 

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.900
the proper controls recommended.

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:59.840
After controls recommended by the Health Department
have been introduced, a stream of water keeps

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:01.730
the dust down.

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:08.730
Keeps it out of the air - out of the worker's
lungs.

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:18.720
Bagging powdered mineral is another industrial
operation in which dust is a danger.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:22.760
The engineer comes to the plant to check and
analyze the hazard and make recommendations

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:28.200
that will lead to its removal.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:40.480
The operation is made safe by an exhaust system
which draws off the dust 

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:42.560
and leaves the air clear.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:47.030
The engineer tests the exhaust system to see
if it draws strongly enough to take dust out

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:48.020
of the room.

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:53.530
The powder that seeps out of the bag no longer
fills the room like a cloud, but is sucked

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:56.770
into the exhaust.

00:09:56.770 --> 00:10:02.060
But there are conditions that cause illness
that are harder to find and harder to correct.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:05.880
And here, too, the industrial hygiene units
do their work.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:10.370
They analyze gases, chemicals, and metals,

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:17.370
investigate the causes of sickness among workers.
And the simpler things like recommending goggles

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.940
and gloves and machine guards.

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:25.950
That, too, is part of their job.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:35.020
State health departments and the Public Health
Service are determined not to let ill health

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:38.010
become a bottleneck in this crisis.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:42.450
To fight this danger they're expanding their
work in industrial hygiene.

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:46.870
Some state units are building trucks that
will make it possible to take the laboratory

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:49.120
and the clinic right into the field.

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:52.920
To the gates of the factory, to the mouth
of the mine.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:55.850
In these trucks, material can be tested.

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:58.710
Men x-rayed and examined thoroughly.

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:02.950
Whatever the causes of ill health, all must
fight to wipe them out.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:06.750
Federal and state officials. Industry and workers.

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:09.540
To industry, the Public Health Service says:

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:13.440
"Follow the recommendations of the Industrial
Hygiene Division of your 

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:15.260
state department of health."

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:16.790
To the men on the job:

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:21.020
"Find the safe way to do it, and do it the
safe way."

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:23.420
That is the way to save a day.

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:29.410
Save a day to keep him rolling!

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:34.640
Save a day to keep him flying!

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:42.040
Save a day that Americans of tomorrow may
live in a land of peace, in a land where freedom reigns.

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:49.200
[The End]

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:53.980
[U.S. Public Health Service. 1798]

